1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
A ship’s manifest is a document with
lines. In the database that is two tables.

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:18,600
In Java you want a Manifest that owns a
list of CargoLine — and when a clerk
deletes a line from the list, you want the
row gone without a separate delete call.
That is relationship mapping with orphan
removal.

3
00:00:18,719 --> 00:00:34,128
JPA association annotations describe
cardinality and ownership. @OneToMany /
@ManyToOne cover the manifest↔line case.
@ManyToMany and @OneToOne exist too;
misuse them and join tables multiply.

4
00:00:34,247 --> 00:00:48,359
For harbor cargo, start with the classic
parent-child: Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.
mappedBy = "manifest" says the child owns
the foreign key column manifest_id.

5
00:00:48,479 --> 00:00:52,991
Cascade ALL means persisting a new
manifest persists new lines.

6
00:00:53,111 --> 00:01:06,024
orphanRemoval = true means removing a line
from the collection schedules a DELETE for
that cargo row on flush — the relational
mirror of "this line no longer belongs to
the document." Always maintain both sides
in addLin

7
00:01:06,143 --> 00:01:08,447
e / removeLine.

8
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:17,328
If you only lines.add(line) and forget
line.setManifest(this), the foreign key
may stay null depending on flush order and
owning side rules.

9
00:01:17,447 --> 00:01:27,959
Prefer FetchType.LAZY on collections so
loading one manifest for a header screen
does not pull every line until you ask.
Bidirectional relationships are not free.

10
00:01:28,079 --> 00:01:41,447
Equals/hashCode on entities with generated
ids is a footgun inside sets. Cascading
REMOVE from manifest to lines is powerful
— deleting a manifest deletes its lines;
do that only when the business rule
matches.

11
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:51,480
@ManyToMany between vessels and ports
often wants an explicit association entity
(PortCall) instead of a bare join table,
because the call has its own arrival time.

12
00:01:51,599 --> 00:02:00,191
People mark every association EAGER "so I
never see LazyInitializationException" and
then wonder why a simple manifest list
issues a forest of joins.

13
00:02:00,311 --> 00:02:07,727
Others forget orphan removal and leave
cargo rows pointing at deleted manifests —
or delete lines manually in three places.

14
00:02:07,847 --> 00:02:17,544
A third mistake is putting @JoinColumn on
both sides of a bidirectional pair and
confusing Hibernate about ownership.
Associations give you a graph.

15
00:02:17,663 --> 00:02:24,575
Querying that graph without dropping to
table columns — "manifests for this vessel
on this date" — is JPQL’s job.
